 ## Overview
-Random Camera Sampling is a method for generating a fixed set of camera poses by uniformly selecting from a pre-existing stack of viewpoints [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of initializing training views for neural rendering or 3D reconstruction when a diverse set of source images is available but no specific trajectory is required. It differs from its sibling [Random Pose Selection](8187-random-pose-selection.md) by drawing from a discrete, finite list of existing cameras rather than generating continuous poses from a parametric distribution [S1, unofficial].
+Random Camera Sampling selects a fixed number of cameras from a predefined viewpoint stack by uniformly sampling indices without replacement [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of generating a diverse set of training views for 3D reconstruction or novel view synthesis when a limited pool of existing captures is available [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [Random Pose Selection](8187-random-pose-selection.md) by drawing exclusively from a discrete, existing list of poses rather than generating continuous pose parameters from a distribution [S1, unofficial].
 
